The value of acceleration due to gravity at height $(h=10 \mathrm{~km})$ is $x$.
$\begin{array}{ll}
\text { i.e., } & g_{h}=x \\
\Rightarrow \quad & \frac{g}{\left(1+\frac{h}{R_{e}}\right)^{2}}=x \\
\Rightarrow \quad & g\left(1-\frac{2 h}{R_{e}}\right)=x ...(i)
\end{array}
The value of acceleration due to gravity at depth $d$ below the surface of earth,
$\begin{array}{r}
g_{d}=x \\
g\left(1-\frac{d}{R_{e}}\right)=x ...(ii)
\end{array}
From Eqs. (i) and (ii), we have
$\Rightarrow \quad g\left(1-\frac{2 h}{R_{e}}\right)=g\left(1-\frac{d}{R_{e}}\right)\Rightarrow \quad 1-\frac{2 h}{R_{e}}=1-\frac{d}{R_{e}} \Rightarrow \frac{2 h}{R_{e}}=\frac{d}{R_{e}}\Rightarrow \quad d=2 h=2 \times 10=20 \mathrm{~km}$

Initial angular velocity of wheel, $\omega_{0}=0$
Final angular velocity, $\omega=10 \mathrm{rad} / \mathrm{s}t=5 \mathrm{~s}$
By first equation of rotational motion,
$\begin{array}{ll} 
& \omega=\omega_{0}+\alpha t \\
\Rightarrow \quad & 10=0+\alpha \times 5 \\
\Rightarrow & \alpha=\frac{10}{5}=2 \mathrm{rad} / \mathrm{s}^{2}
\end{array}
If $\theta$ be the total angle through which wheel has turned, then from,
$\begin{aligned}
\theta &=\omega_{0} t+\frac{1}{2} \alpha t^{2} \\
&=0 \times 5+\frac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 5^{2}=0+25 \\
\Rightarrow \quad \theta &=25 \mathrm{rad}
\end{aligned}
